-- title:
--   Evaluation onto invariant meromorphic functions is surjective
-- statement:
--   The statement concerns a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient together with an analytic uniformisation of its points, and asserts that a ring homomorphism from the function field of the base-changed curve to the field of $\Gamma'$-invariant meromorphic functions on Drinfeld's upper half-plane, compatible with constants and with evaluation at uniformised points, is surjective.
--
--   **Arithmetic base.** A prime $r$ is fixed. $\mathcal O$ is a domain of characteristic zero, assumed by `hdvr` to be a discrete valuation ring; $\pi\in\mathcal O$ is irreducible (`hπ`); $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete (`hcomplete`); the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (`hres`); and the ideals $(r)$ and $(\pi)$ of $\mathcal O$ coincide (`hunr`). $K_0$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficient ring.** $O^{nr}$ is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal O$-algebra, with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $O^{nr}$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{nr}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{nr}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}$ modulo $\pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Determinant valuation.** $v\!\det\colon \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) satisfies `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g=u\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Group data.** $G$ is a group, $\sigma\colon G\to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and $\rho\colon G\to \mathrm{PGL}(2,K_0)$ are homomorphisms with $\rho(g)$ the class of $\sigma(g)$ for all $g$ (`hρ`), and $\Gamma\le G$ is a subgroup. The hypothesis `hcent` provides $z\in\Gamma$ with $\sigma(z)$ a scalar matrix $c\cdot 1$ and $v\!\det(\sigma z)=2$; `hodd` provides $w\in\Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w)=1$. The subgroup $\Gamma'\le G$ is characterised by `hΓ'`: $x\in\Gamma'$ exactly when $x\in\Gamma$ and $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ is even. Three further conditions are imposed on the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acting on the set of vertices of the lattice tree of $\mathcal O$ in $K_0^2$ (homothety classes of full lattices): `hvtf` (virtual torsion-freeness) gives a subgroup $\Gamma''\le\Gamma'$ of non-zero relative index in $\Gamma'$ all of whose elements of finite order in $\rho(\Gamma'')$ are trivial; `hdisc` requires every vertex stabiliser in $\rho(\Gamma')$ to be finite; `hcocpt` requires a finite set $S$ of vertices such that every vertex is carried into $S$ by some element of $\rho(\Gamma')$.
--
--   **The quotient datum.** $\mathcal X$ is a scheme and $f\colon\mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is proper and flat; `hsmooth` states that the projection to $\operatorname{Spec}K_0$ of the base change of $f$ along $\operatorname{Spec}K_0\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$. Write $\Omega$ for the Drinfeld functor `Omega K₀ π`, sending an $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ to the set of Deligne data over $B$ (a $B$-submodule $\mathrm{line}(M)\subseteq B\otimes_{\mathcal O}M$ for every full lattice $M\subseteq K_0^2$, with invertible quotients, compatible with inclusions of lattices, equivariant for homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$), and write $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal O}(O^{nr},-)$ for the functor corepresented by $O^{nr}$. The functor `Scheme.nilpPoints f` sends $B$ to the sections $\operatorname{Spec}B\to\mathcal X$ of $f$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. Then $\Theta$ assigns, to every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map $\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal O}(O^{nr},B)\times\Omega(B)\to (\text{nilpotent-}\pi\text{ points of }f)(B)$, subject to four conditions. `hΘnat`: $\Theta$ is natural in $B$. `hΘinv`: $\Theta$ is invariant under the twisted $\Gamma$-action, i.e. if $\gamma\in\Gamma$ and $x,x'$ satisfy `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for $\sigma(\gamma)$ — meaning $x'_1=x_1\circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det(\sigma\gamma)}$ and $x'_2$ is the pullback of $x_2$ along $\sigma(\gamma)^{-1}$, lattice by lattice — then $\Theta(x')=\Theta(x)$. `hΘfib`: for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with the image of $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi\colon O^{nr}\to k$, every point of $f$ over $k$ is $\Theta_k(\psi,P)$ for some $P\in\Omega(k)$, and $\Theta_k(\psi,P)=\Theta_k(\psi,P')$ holds exactly when $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the twisted action of some $\sigma(\gamma)$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma$. `hΘuniv`: $\Theta$ is universal among such data — for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of maps into the nilpotent-$\pi$ points of $T$ which is natural and twisted-$\Gamma$-invariant in the same two senses, there is a natural family $u$ from the nilpotent-$\pi$ points of $f$ to those of $T$ with $u\circ\Theta=\rho'$, and every natural family $u'$ with $u'\circ\Theta=\rho'$ agrees with $u$ at every argument.
--
--   **Analytic data.** $C$ is an algebraically closed $K_0$-algebra field, complete for a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$; $R$ is a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra with $C$ an $R$-algebra, compatibly with the $\mathcal O$- and $K_0$-structures on $C$. $\varpi$ is a pseudo-uniformiser: an element $\varpi\in K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$, with the scaling property that every non-zero $a\in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N\le v(a)\le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$. The hypothesis `hF` states that $(\pi,\varpi,R)$ is an adic frame: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image the set of elements of valuation $\le 1$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, $\mathcal O$ is the set of elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ in $C$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Further: $\psi_0\colon O^{nr}\to R$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map; `hrk` requires that for $x,y\in C$ with $v(x)<1$ and $y\neq 0$ there is $n$ with $v(x)^n\le v(y)$; `hval` requires that for every $\varepsilon\neq 0$ in $\Gamma_0$ there is $y\neq 0$ in $C$ with $v(y)\le\varepsilon$; `htame` requires that for every vertex $v$ the cardinality of the stabiliser $\{g\in\rho(\Gamma') : g\cdot v=v\}$ has valuation $1$ in $C$; `hex` requires that every point of the upper half-plane $\Omega_C=C\setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids attached to $\varpi$; and the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` — the functions on $\Omega_C$ which on each affinoid are uniform limits of uniformly bounded pole-free rational functions — is assumed to be a domain.
--
--   **Uniformisation.** $\Phi$ is a map from `AdicPoint K₀ π R` — compatible systems of Deligne data over the rings $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ — to the sections $\operatorname{Spec}R\to\mathcal X$ of $f$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. The hypothesis `hΦ` has four parts: (i) for every adic point $x$ and every $n$, the reduction of $\Phi(x)$ along $R\to R/(\pi^{n+1})$ equals $\Theta$ applied to the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ reduced modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ and the $n$-th term $x.\mathrm{pt}(n)$ of $x$; (ii) $\Phi$ is surjective; (iii) $\Phi(x)=\Phi(x')$ if and only if $x'=x\cdot\sigma(\gamma)$ for some $\gamma\in\Gamma'$; (iv) composition with $\operatorname{Spec}C\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ is injective on these $R$-sections, and every $C$-point $\operatorname{Spec}C\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ arises from such an $R$-section.
--
--   **The base change and the homomorphism.** $s_C\colon\operatorname{Spec}C\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ (`hsC`), and the fibre product $\mathcal X_C$ of $f$ along $s_C$ is assumed integral. $e$ is a ring homomorphism from the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ to the subfield `Mumford.invariantFieldOf C G (Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ) Γ'` of $\operatorname{Frac}$ of the holomorphic function ring consisting of the elements fixed by every $\gamma\in\Gamma'$ for the $G$-action carried by `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ`. Two compatibilities are imposed. `he₀`: $e$ is $C$-linear, in that for every $c\in C$ the image under $e$ of the canonical image of $c$ in the function field (the germ at the generic point coming from the structure morphism $\mathcal X_C\to\operatorname{Spec}C$) is the image of $c$ under the algebra map into the invariant field. `heval`: for every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point and every section $s$ of the structure sheaf over $U$, there are $a,b$ in the holomorphic function ring with $b$ a non-zero-divisor such that the image under $e$ of the germ of $s$ at the generic point equals $a/b$ in $\operatorname{Frac}$ of that ring, and moreover for every adic point $x$, every $z$ in the upper half-plane with $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}(C)$, and every morphism $q\colon\operatorname{Spec}C\to\mathcal X_C$ whose composition with the first projection is $\operatorname{Spec}C\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ followed by $\Phi(x)$ and whose composition with the second projection is the identity, if $q^{-1}U$ is the whole of $\operatorname{Spec}C$ then $b(z)$ times the value at $q$ of the section $s$ (the global section of $\operatorname{Spec}C$ obtained by pulling $s$ back along $q$, read as an element of $C$) equals $a(z)$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under all of the above, $e$ is surjective: every $\Gamma'$-invariant element of $\operatorname{Frac}$ of the ring of holomorphic functions on $\Omega_C$ is the image under $e$ of a rational function on $\mathcal X_C$.
--
--   This is the surjectivity half of the identification of the function field of the generic fibre of a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient with the field of $\Gamma'$-invariant meromorphic functions on the Drinfeld upper half-plane, in the Mumford-style analytic description of the quotient. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringEquiv_functionField_pullback_invariantFieldOf_smul_level_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_tame_of_virtuallyTorsionFree_of_smooth`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringEquiv_functionField_pullback_invariantFieldOf_smul_level_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_tame_of_virtuallyTorsionFree_of_smooth) to upgrade the evaluation homomorphism $e$ to a ring isomorphism.
